<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xie Lingyun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">385–433</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xie Lingyun was a Chinese poet of the Eastern Jin and Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties, a member of the aristocratic Xie clan who held the title Duke of Kangle. He is regarded as the founder of Chinese landscape poetry (shanshui), describing the mountains and rivers of the south from his travels. A devout Buddhist, he also wrote on Buddhist doctrine, but his arrogance led to banishment and he was executed for rebellion in 433.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xie-lingyun.jpg" alt="Xie Lingyun" width="314" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanō Tsunenobu (1636-1713) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_and_Northern_Dynasties_poet_Xie_Lingyun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
